@@ -0,0 +1,478 @@
+# Architecture — Reliability Copilot
+
+## Architecture Overview
+
+This is a **Reliability Copilot**: a single-domain reliability-engineering assistant built around a FastAPI host and one MCP (Model Context Protocol) server. The architecture separates concerns:
+
+- **Host** (`backend/src/host/`): FastAPI web server, LLM orchestration, MCP client pool - assembles context, enforces grounding, holds no behavior rules
+- **Reliability domain** (`backend/src/domains/reliability/`): MCP server with tools, system prompt, and knowledge base - owns all reliability behavior
+- **Frontend** (`frontend/`): React chat UI that communicates only via HTTP API
+
+### Key Design Principles
+
+1. **Single domain**: The app serves reliability engineering only. There is no domain selection, no domain registry, and no per-domain switching in code, config, or UI.
+2. **MCP Protocol**: All computational tools are exposed as MCP tools (stdio or HTTP transport). The LLM calls tools; Python computes results.
+3. **Local-First**: Knowledge base, embeddings, and static files live on disk by default. Cloud migration is optional.
+4. **Security by Default**: API keys encrypted at rest, SSRF protection, rate limiting, no secrets in config files.
+
+## Directory Structure
+
+```
+reliability-copilot/
+├── backend/
+│   ├── src/
+│   │   ├── host/              # FastAPI host (context assembly, grounding guard)
+│   │   │   ├── app.py         # Main application, chat endpoints, LLM orchestration
+│   │   │   ├── mcp_pool.py    # MultiMCPClient (stdio + SSE + streamable HTTP)
+│   │   │   └── routes/        # API route handlers
+│   │   ├── common/            # Shared utilities (credential_store, rate_limit, markdown_math)
+│   │   ├── knowledge/         # GenericKnowledgeBase (Chroma + BM25 RAG)
+│   │   └── domains/           # Reliability domain (the only domain)
+│   │       └── reliability/   # Reliability engineering domain
+│   │           ├── server.py  # MCP server (standalone or via host)
+│   │           ├── tools/     # Tool implementations (weibull, blife, report, knowledge)
+│   │           ├── plugins/   # Extensible tool plugins (mtbf.py)
+│   │           └── prompt.md  # Reliability system prompt (sole behavior source)
+│   ├── config/                # MCP server list
+│   └── mcp_servers.reliability.json
+│   ├── static/                # Plots, reports, built UI
+│   └── tests/                 # Backend tests
+├── frontend/
+│   ├── src/
+│   │   ├── components/        # React components (ChatPage, Composer, SettingsPanel, etc.)
+│   │   ├── services/          # Service layer (BackendService, PlatformService interfaces)
+│   │   ├── hooks/             # React hooks (useSessions, useTheme)
+│   │   ├── types/             # TypeScript types (chat, llm)
+│   │   └── utils/             # Utilities (format, mathMarkdown)
+│   └── package.json
+└── knowledge-base/            # Domain documents (reliability handbooks, procedures)
+```
+
+## Reliability Domain Layout
+
+The only domain lives at `backend/src/domains/reliability/`:
+
+- `server.py`: MCP server with `@mcp.tool()` decorated functions
+- `tools/`: Tool implementation files (weibull, B-life, MTBF, KM, distributions, EDA, growth, knowledge, report)
+- `plugins/`: Optional plugins with `def register(mcp):` hooks (`mtbf.py`)
+- `prompt.md`: Reliability system prompt — the sole source of behavior rules (routing, refusals, reply shape)
+
+The host loads the prompt and the MCP server list (`backend/config/mcp_servers.reliability.json`, or `MCP_SERVERS_CONFIG` override) directly. There is no domain manifest and no registry.
+
+Knowledge base (single): documents in `knowledge-base/`, Chroma collection `reliability_knowledge`, index in `backend/.rag/`.
+
+### Single-Agent Behavior
+
+The frontend sends `prompt` plus transcript `history` with every `/api/chat`
+and `/api/chat/stream` request; there is no mode field and no mode state
+machine. All behavior rules live in `prompt.md` — the host only assembles
+context per turn:
+
+- Delimited full-session transcript plus a compact ledger (established
+  values, methods, dataset, plot filenames, units) from the second turn on.
+- KB context for questions (skipped for context-bound follow-ups that run
+  from transcript+ledger).
+- One-line request-type hints: method questions ("how is B10 calculated?")
+  get an explanation contract, interpretation questions ("which do I trust?")
+  get an answer-from-context contract.
+
+One rule is enforced in code (`stream_checked_agent_events`): when the
+current request gave numeric data but no tool ran, the reply is repaired
+once (demanding computation or a clarifying question); an error is emitted
+only if the repaired reply still reports unsupported new numbers. Transcript
+digits never trigger this guard, so restating established values is never a
+violation. Method questions (how-calculated/formula) and interpretation
+questions are never guarded: digits like "B10" or "63.2%" in an explanation
+request are not a data turn, so no repair and no requirements error can fire. Number-free replies (e.g. asking for missing units) stream
+untouched with no repair and no error.
+
+### Agent Transitions
+
+One agent covers every turn — the user just asks, and history carries
+established context forward for follow-ups:
+
+| You say | What the agent does |
+|---|---|
+| Ask a question | Answers from reliability expertise (plus KB context), citing sources when used |
+| Ask how something is calculated / its formula | Explains the method from KB context when present, else from its own reliability-engineering training — never refuses for lack of failure data |
+| Ask which result to trust / why results differ | Explains from expertise and established values — restating conversation values needs no tools, never an input checklist |
+| Ask something unrelated to the analysis | Treated as a fresh request (tools/KB available); prior context kept as background only |
+| Give failure data | Computes with the tools (or asks for missing units) — never invents numbers |
+
+### Domain System Prompt (prompt.md)
+
+The prompt should:
+- Never invent numeric results (β, η, MTBF, B-life, etc.)
+- Always call MCP tools for computations
+- Use search tools for handbook/procedure questions
+- Cite knowledge base sources when provided
+- Use LaTeX for mathematics
+- Answer in technically rigorous Markdown
+
+Example:
+```
+You are a Reliability Copilot: a local assistant for reliability engineering. Never invent numeric lives (MTBF, B10/B20/B30, β, η). Always call MCP tools (analyze_weibull, calculate_b_life, calculate_mtbf, export_reliability_report) for those quantities. If a tool cannot be run, say you cannot give a number. Use search_reliability_knowledge for procedures, handbook questions, and any factual who/what question about people, documents, or local notes. When local knowledge-base context is provided with the question, use it and cite its source. When a plot is created, mention its filename and summarize β, η, sample (failures vs suspensions), method (RRY or MLE), bounds if present, and meaning. For warranty questions, compare B10/B20/B30 to the warranty period using tool output. Use LaTeX for mathematics and answer in technically rigorous Markdown.
+```
+
+## MCP Tool Development
+
+### Tool Registration
+
+Tools are registered in the domain's `server.py`:
+
+```python
+from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer
+
+mcp = MCPServer("reliability-engineering")
+
+@mcp.tool()
+def my_tool(param: float) -> dict[str, Any]:
+    """Tool description in plain language."""
+    return {"result": param * 2}
+```
+
+### Tool Implementation
+
+Implement tools in `tools/` directory:
+
+```python
+# backend/src/domains/reliability/tools/my_tool.py
+def my_tool(param: float) -> dict[str, Any]:
+    """Implementation with validation and computation."""
+    if param < 0:
+        raise ValueError("param must be non-negative")
+    result = perform_calculation(param)
+    return {"result": result}
+```
+
+### Plugin System
+
+Add plugins in `plugins/` directory:
+
+```python
+# backend/src/domains/reliability/plugins/my_plugin.py
+def register(mcp):
+    @mcp.tool()
+    def plugin_tool(x: float) -> float:
+        """Auto-loaded by server.py on startup."""
+        return x * 2
+```
+
+The server automatically loads all plugins via `load_plugins(mcp)`.
+
+### Tool Return Values
+
+- Return JSON-serializable dicts (use `json_safe()` for NumPy types)
+- For plots: save to `STATIC_DIR/` and return filename
+- MCP clients (Unsloth Studio, VS Code) can use `Image()` blocks
+- Our UI: backend infers plot URL from response text
+
+### MCP Transports
+
+Domains support three transports:
+
+1. **stdio** (default): Spawned as subprocess by host (local dev)
+2. **sse**: Server-Sent Events HTTP endpoint
+3. **streamable-http**: HTTP streaming endpoint
+
+Run standalone:
+```bash
+python -m src.domains.reliability.server --transport streamable-http --port 8001
+# Tools at http://localhost:8001/mcp
+```
+
+## Security Requirements
+
+### P0 Security (Never bypass)
+
+1. **APP_TOKEN**: Required for mutating endpoints in network deployments
+   - `PUT /api/llm/config`
+   - `POST /api/llm/test`
+   - `POST /api/knowledge/upload`
+   - Generate with: `python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"`
+
+2. **Credential Storage**:
+   - API keys NEVER stored in `.llm_config.json`
+   - API keys NEVER returned by GET endpoints
+   - API keys NEVER written to localStorage/sessionStorage
+   - Encrypted at rest in `backend/.llm_secret.enc` (Fernet)
+   - Encryption key in `backend/.credential.key` (0600) or `CREDENTIAL_KEY` env
+
+3. **SSRF Protection**:
+   - `ALLOW_PRIVATE_LLM=true` (default) allows localhost/LAN LLMs
+   - Cloud deployments MUST set `ALLOW_PRIVATE_LLM=false`
+   - Metadata endpoints always denied
+
+4. **Host Binding**:
+   - Default: `HOST=127.0.0.1` (loopback only)
+   - Set `HOST=0.0.0.0` only behind authenticated TLS proxy
+
+### Rate Limiting
+
+- Per IP, in-memory (also limit at ingress in cloud)
+- `RATE_LIMIT_API_PER_MIN=120` (default)
+- `RATE_LIMIT_SENSITIVE_PER_MIN=10` (config, upload, test)
+
+### Multi-User Authentication (Cloud)
+
+- `AUTH_MODE=proxy-header`: Trust header from loopback ingress sidecar
+- `TRUST_PROXY_USER_HEADER=X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL` (Azure Easy Auth)
+- Per-user credential namespacing (hashed user IDs)
+- `SECRET_BACKEND=azure-keyvault` for cloud secret management
+- `AZURE_KEY_VAULT_URL` with managed identity
