@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+# Life-Data Methods Map (generic workflow + NIST Handbook, synthesis)
+
+Paraphrased planning reference. Consult the primary sources for authoritative
+detail. Nothing here is copied from licensed texts.
+
+## 1. The four-step workflow (standard life data analysis)
+
+1. **Gather life data** for the product (times plus censoring codes).
+2. **Select a lifetime distribution** that fits the data and models product life.
+3. **Estimate the parameters** of the selected distribution (RRY or MLE).
+4. **Generate plots and results** that estimate the life characteristics
+   (reliability, mean life, B-lives, failure rate).
+
+## 2. Distribution families in scope
+
+Parametric families covered by this repository's fitting engine:
+
+- Exponential, Weibull, Normal, Lognormal, Logistic, Loglogistic, Gumbel/SEV
+  (minima form for life data).
+
+Companion topics for further study (pointers, not implemented here):
+Life Data Classification, Confidence Bounds, Non-Parametric Analysis,
+Competing Failure Modes, Warranty Data Analysis, Recurrent Events,
+Degradation Data Analysis, Reliability Test Design.
+
+## 3. Non-parametric empirical reference (NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook, Chapter 8)
+
+Chapter 8, *Assessing Product Reliability*, covers censored life data, life
+distributions, and estimation. Section 8.2.1.5 specifies the empirical,
+distribution-free Kaplan-Meier approach for right-censored data: the
+product-limit survival curve with Greenwood confidence bounds is the
+model-free reference against which parametric families are judged.
+
+Source: NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods (public domain) —
+<https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/>. Full Chapters 1 and 8 are indexed
+locally in this knowledge base (`nist-eda-exploratory-data-analysis.md`,
+`nist-apr-assessing-product-reliability.md`); prefer citing those chunks.
+
+## 4. Selection discipline used here
+
+- Fit every candidate family by right-censored MLE (same engine, no forks).
+- Rank by AIC (lowest first); treat gaps under ~2 points as ties, not wins.
+- Cross-check with the Kaplan-Meier hazard-shape cue (increasing /
+  constant / decreasing); unresolved disagreements are flagged, never hidden.
+- `selection="single"` commits to the lowest-AIC family; `"multi"` returns
+  the ranked shortlist with the KM overlay for comparison first.
+
+## 5. Tool mapping in this repository
+
+| Workflow step | MCP tool |
+|---|---|
+| Empirical KM reference (non-parametric) | `analyze_nonparametric_life_data` |
+| Fit one family (any of the seven) | `fit_life_distribution` |
+| Rank/select one or many families | `select_life_distribution` |
+| Weibull probability paper + bounds | `analyze_weibull` |
+| B-life percentiles (Weibull) | `calculate_b_life` |
+| Structured plan (data check, KM, select, fit, report) | `create_life_data_plan` |
+| Grounded Q&A evidence pack | `ask_life_data_question` |
